Patch 09b: transform partition stationarity pruning, cheaper and more
aggressive. Supersedes patch 09 (0009-tx-partition-stationarity-pruning.patch)
-- apply this INSTEAD of it, not on top.

CTC round 1 measured patch 09 as the only patch clearing the bar on both
classes at Speed 4: +2.60% / +0.11% BD-YUV on A1 (ratio 23.6) and
+2.27% / +0.09% on A2 (25.2), against a bar of 20. At slower presets it fell
away, and on A1 at Speed 3 it was a net slowdown of -0.27%.

Two changes, addressing those two facts separately.

1. The stationarity measurement is now lazy, and its test runs last.

   It was computed unconditionally before the candidate loop, and its check
   ran before the cheap eligibility tests. So the residual walk was paid for
   candidates that skip_tx_partition_by_eval_type and use_tx_partition were
   about to reject anyway, and on blocks where every split gets eliminated by
   other speed features -- where it can never fire, and is pure cost. That is
   the most likely source of the -0.27% at Speed 3 on 4K, where more blocks
   are visited and more of them have their splits pruned by other features
   first. It is now computed on first genuine use, after all the cheap checks,
   and skipped entirely when the only surviving candidate is
   TX_PARTITION_NONE. This should be a strict improvement: same decisions,
   less work.

2. TX_PART_STATIONARITY_MARGIN goes from 4 to 2, loosening the stationarity
   test from "strongest strip within 1.25x of mean" to "within 1.50x".

   Clearing a bar of 20 with a ratio of 23-25 while costing about a tenth of a
   percent of BD-rate means the original threshold sat far inside the safe
   region with speedup left unclaimed. If the ratio merely held near 23, an
   operating point at +0.33% BD would be worth roughly +8% speedup and would
   still pass. The ratio will decay as the test loosens; where it crosses 20
   is exactly what a sweep has to find.

Both tunables are now overridable at build time, so a sweep needs no patch
edits: -DTX_PART_STATIONARITY_MARGIN=3, and so on. Change 1 is expected to be
safe. Change 2 is a deliberate step along an unmeasured curve -- if you want
only the free part, build with -DTX_PART_STATIONARITY_MARGIN=4 to keep the
measured decisions while still getting the overhead fix.

